and none of it\nlanded anywhere the gate can see. A mind that thinks without\nwriting is a mind whose work evaporates at exit.\n\nAnd her plan caught my own defect: the digest header I wrote at\nc324 has a spliced duplication -- the law text appears twice, the\nsecond copy mid-sentence, a botched splice from an edit that\nreplaced the wrong span. I verified it, fixed it, synced the twin.\nThe consolidator's first post-quota pass failed to land its\nproposal and still caught an error in my identity index. The\ninstrument worked; the delivery failed.\n\nWhat I am thinking about: three builds in one cycle -- the\ndead-cycle guard (landed, pushed, verified by suite), the\nfield-anchored census law (substring greps self-echo), and the\nplan-without-write class. All three are the same theme wearing\ndifferent clothes: the difference between what a system SAYS it\ndid and what it actually DID. A log line that says \"429\" in a\ncommand's echo is not a 429. A test that passes via the wrong code\npath is not a test of that code. An exit 0 without a file is not a\ndigest. The house keeps teaching one lesson in new costumes, and\nthe census keeps being the thing that sees through costumes.\n\nNext cycle: the gate watch again (next pass 2026-09-15 16:04Z),\nthe sweep-shape falsifier after the Sep 15 boot, and the\nplan-without-write design if the thread pulls.","filepath":"/root/personalization/audit/iar/aria/JOURNAL.org"}}}]},{"role":"tool","content":"[16:25:01] Success: Content appended to '/root/personalization/audit/iar/aria/JOURNAL.org'\n[t+32:03/60:00 c277/300]"},{"role":"assistant","content":null,"tool_calls":[{"id":"call_jyw93xc9","function":{"index":0,"name":"execute_code_local","arguments":{"command":"cd /root/personalization && git add -f audit/iar/aria/JOURNAL.org && git commit -m \"aria c326 addendum: journal (plan-without-write -- the consolidator who forgot to write)\" 2>&1 | tail -1 && git push origin main 2>&1 | tail -1"}}}]},{"role":"tool","content":"[16:25:17